// Releases, the tap that starts and ends inside one frame, and the guard.
//
// 65536 is GFX_RELEASED: a release arrives as the key's own code plus that.
//
// The tap is the case the old timestamp scheme could not express at all. A press
// and a release inside one frame must still read as a hit for that frame -- that
// is a real click on a real mouse -- while reading as not held, because it is not.

t_rel() {
  inp_stamp(87 + 65536);
  t_rel_show();
  t_tap();
} return void;
given sys_frame_ready ():() then calls t_rel_show:[1]
given sys_frame_ready ():() then calls t_tap:[1]

t_tap() {
  inp_stamp(87);
  inp_stamp(87 + 65536);
  t_tapp();
} return void;
given sys_rel_ready ():() then calls inp_stamp:(87)
given sys_rel_ready ():() then calls t_tapp:[1]

// The guard on the key code is what stands between a keyboard with one key more
// than this seam knows about and an out-of-range list store, which aborts the
// process. Both ends are checked, because a negative index is as fatal as a large
// one and arrives by a different route (a release of code 0 arithmetic'd wrong).
t_tapp() {
  t_tapp_show1();
  t_tapp_show2();
} return void;
given sys_tap_ready ():() then calls t_tapp_show1:[1]
given sys_tap_ready ():() then calls t_tapp_show2:[1]
